WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.550
It's great to welcome back to the program Michael Wood Jr.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:10.409
Who's a police management expert, activist, president of Veterans Stand as well as former Baltimore police commander

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:15.630
Michael, it's great to have you back on the program and the timing really couldn't be better although

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.080
I don't know if 'better' is the right word to use here

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.960
You reacted to the speech that Donald Trump gave in front of a law-enforcement crowd

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.540
where he sort of jokingly said, you know it's really not a bad idea to kind of rough up suspects that you have in custody

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:37.560
if they've been accused or are suspected of having committed a serious enough crime

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.639
Which to me- it was disturbing on so many levels

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:48.209
Not the least of which that being arrested for something is not the same as having been convicted

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:52.649
Of something regardless of how serious it is. So take it from the top

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:55.619
I mean, what was your reaction to the comments from President Trump?

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.759
Well, thanks for having me again, and I mean the basic

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.699
Idea that's really bothering me when it comes to what Trump is saying is that

00:01:05.979 --> 00:01:12.659
That's the seed that we kind of have in our culture that we aren't looking at the command structure

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:20.520
We aren't looking at the actual power that's setup incentivizing all this and setting these balls in motion. What we end up doing is we

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:24.380
Blame the officer that actually bangs the head on the door

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.699
But this whole thing is a part of a culture

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:33.599
That comes down and when you are the president then you are talking down to police officers

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:40.100
or whoever's out on the street to execute your will, to go and do something that is against the basic premise of

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.080
professional law enforcement even if- even as bad American law enforcement is

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.740
there is not a best practices book or police expert out there that's going to say you treat

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:53.609
Convicted suspects this way let alone

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:01.049
Citizens who are proven innocent or innocent until proven guilty so there- there is this systemic

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.170
attitude where policing ends up

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.980
Encapsulating like what America really is and

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.600
I'm anti Trump just as much as I'm anti sneezing

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:19.499
But I don't blame the sneeze for my sneeze, like he is a product of us and if we can't see that that this

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:20.980
really demonstrates

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:25.140
What America is in its soul and we need to fight that

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:28.140
Then I don't really know what people are looking at

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:33.580
There's a similar idea that we sometimes hear when it comes to the

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:39.840
sentencing of individuals convicted of child sex abuse and some other crimes sometimes- this idea that

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.820
Hey, their punishment will be made worse because they might get sexually assaulted

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:48.500
in our prisons or in our jails. And when I hear that I say

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:53.040
That's not something that we should be applauding because it's going to make their time worse

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.230
That's sort of an inability of our

00:02:55.659 --> 00:02:57.719
prison system to keep individuals

00:02:57.909 --> 00:03:04.019
safe like the fact that you might be sexually assaulted in prison depending on the crime that you're convicted of

00:03:04.299 --> 00:03:10.289
That's something that we should be concerned about not cheering as part of some additional justice

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.389
Is there similarity between these two ideas?

00:03:12.909 --> 00:03:15.119
We should be incredibly concerned

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:22.530
And I'm really happy you brought that up and have made that connection that you see in our prison system and again that is

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:28.799
America right we say to ourselves. Let's punish this person. Let's put them into a system

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:32.249
Where, they will have injustice inhumanity

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.879
They will they will be assaulted and have all this punishment

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.489
Go down upon them despite the fact that punishment isn't going to do anything to make the situation better

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:48.479
And then we are trying to like change the prison system and say criminal justice needs to be better

00:03:48.609 --> 00:03:50.609
but at the same time

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:57.000
Applauding when someone we don't like goes into an inhumane system. We do the same thing with police like

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.540
as much as I want to see accountability in policing

00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:06.959
There is no justice in sending a police officer to an inhumane and injust system

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.659
So we have to really look in ourselves

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:14.580
To figure out like what do we really want Policing to be?

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.900
Are we the country that's going to stand up and say we want to be a punitive thing

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.460
We don't care about science despite the fact that every

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:29.600
Meta-analysis of scientific Literature will show you that punishment does not equal a deterrent. That we are still so hell-bent on being a violent society

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:33.779
That wants to even think that these things would work

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:42.800
Like we're going down a pathway that is incredibly concerning to me and obviously it is to you

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.740
You used the word 'systemic'

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:49.780
a little bit earlier in the interview, and I think if we sort of imagine a spectrum

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:58.020
You might have those completely on one side who believe that the issues with police violence and police brutality are 100% systemic

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.139
and that there's very little blame to place on the individual officers. On the other side of that spectrum you might imagine those who

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:10.709
believe this is merely a few bad apples, only the individual police officers are to blame here

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:16.049
and there is nothing systemic going on. The truth is probably sort of somewhere in the middle of that

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:19.109
But I want to ask you, where do you think it is? In other words,

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:23.639
How much of this problem is the individuals? How much of the problem is systemic?

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:32.320
I'm- Well of course I'm throwing out bullshit park number, but I'm going to go with probably 95%

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:39.340
To 99% is completely systemic.  The way I can kind of encapsulate this on

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:44.100
An individual intimate level here is that I grew up

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:50.540
Trying to be raised 'blind'. I mean I grew up in the age of information in mixed neighborhoods

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:56.880
And I had this idea to myself that as a police officer what I was going to do is be impartial

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:02.848
I was going to follow the letter of the law, I was going to follow the departmental policies to a tee and it

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:09.090
Took me a while, but I realized eventually at the end that all of my actions of what I was

00:06:09.909 --> 00:06:14.069
empirically doing on the street was was racist it was

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:21.440
Prejudice against the society, it was inhumane. It was it was in just and all of those things that I was doing?

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:31.340
I was really looking at the system and saying if we do this perfectly, if we do this system perfectly then we will have justice-

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.480
So explain that a little bit like so for example

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:37.740
I mean having interviewed you before ,would one example of what you're talking about be

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.760
Despite the reality that you went in

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:47.800
Wanting to be race blind and not discriminating etc for example by virtue of the neighborhoods you were

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:54.540
policing, the impact of your policing was going to be effectively racist? Is that an example of what you mean?

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:56.640
Yeah, that's a good example

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:02.610
Or whether I put someone into bonds or whether I was looking at so I thought I was a narcotics detective, hunting down

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.560
narcotics, but I wasn't. There was an entire half of that city

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.480
Which we know empirically, without a doubt and anyone can argue it and I know it from practical experience

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:19.110
That white communities have just as much drugs if not more- one reason why cocaine sentencing is much higher is not because of a racist

00:07:19.110 --> 00:07:25.409
Thing intentionally, it's because cocaine is a white drug and so white drugs end up having more resources

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:30.689
so a mid-level dealer ends up being much higher on the scale of being a

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:36.760
'Resource'- same damn word I used a second ago- but so those things that we really end up doing

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.420
We think this is normal

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:43.400
But when we end up going after the drug deal- Where do they put me? They put me in East and West Baltimore

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:44.760
to go after drug dealers

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:51.779
So I end up going after only the community because the drug dealing and drug possession is actually equal if not

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:56.040
More in a rich and white community. Think about it, drugs are expensive

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:02.610
Like they're- I mean people with money have more drugs than people without money that is even is even rational

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:07.529
But where was I sent to go get the drugs? In redlined

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:11.369
Poor neighborhoods to put into an inhumane system that how's about

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.690
System that they can't

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:19.360
Can't do so it's not like- it's not necessarily the officers thought that when they're doing that

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.500
and even if they do it well, they send people into
 a system

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:23.860
So even if you had a police department

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:29.740
That was operating perfectly, they would still be sending people and handing them off to a state's attorney who-

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:35.400
States attorneys are blatantly corrupt around around the country-
They are incentivized in that same system

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:42.359
So they have the same problems, they're incentivized to WIN, not for justice. So like we talked about federal prosecutors ago

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.849
'You better be aware those federal prosecutors. They have a ninety seven percent

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:49.379
Conviction rate' Well, they don't have a ninety seven percent 'conviction' rate they have a 97 percent

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.859
'victory' rate which means that cases are pleaded down or

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:55.340
Whatever they do, they use and manipulate power

00:08:55.340 --> 00:09:00.120
So that they can rise through the ranks. So they don't get incentivized to do the right thing, they get

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:06.860
Incentivized for the most convictions to rise up through the ranks and then prisons are incentivised to be as efficient as possible.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:13.600
So give people as really little resources as possible, why we're doing this punishment system. So it is this whole big

00:09:13.780 --> 00:09:21.820
institution that police officers are a pawn of. It's the people above the street cops that all set this thing into motion and

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:27.520
Don't take any of the responsibility for it. One reason why you see cops getting away with

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.860
committing murders right on camera is

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:33.360
when- that these things Indict the system.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.540
When they indict the system the whole thing will rally around them to protect them and get them off- like Freddie Gray

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:44.380
so when the officers kill Freddie Gray in Baltimore, 
no supervisors took accountability for this

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:48.940
So understanding that this killing- What happened was- is- by the court case anyway

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:53.480
He goes into a van, where they're supposed to be transporting him to a prison cell

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:56.140
They already put them in there for really sketchy reasons to begin with

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:59.720
But then they bring them out of the van dead. 
So the whole time-

00:09:59.720 --> 00:10:03.100
this is a van that doesn't have seatbelts,
 doesn't have proper supervision

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:07.940
doesn't have cameras in the back, 
doesn't have any accountability

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.900
and Freddie Gray was the fourth person to be killed or

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.970
Paralyzed in the back of those vans just in recent history

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:19.429
But no command members
 Kevin Davis doesn't pay a price,
the Mayor doesn't pay a price

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:27.049
Nobody has anything happened to them other than the individual officer at the bottom who was really just a pawn all along

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:32.209
We've been speaking with Michael. Wood Jr. He is a former Baltimore police commander

00:10:32.210 --> 00:10:39.349
You can follow him on Twitter @MichaelAWoodJr and also check out civilianledpolicing.org

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.810
Michael, always great to talk to you

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.989
Yeah, thanks for having me David. Thanks for throwing in those pitches there

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:47.810
We definitely need people to go to Veterans stand and Civilian led policing

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:52.490
That is the way you guys can take something in your own hand and do something in your community

